            "<strong>ARDS</strong> is not a disease but the lung's single response to a severe insult: an inflammatory leak of protein-rich fluid into the alveoli within a week of a known trigger — pneumonia, aspiration, sepsis from any source, severe malaria, major trauma and massive transfusion, pancreatitis, burns and smoke inhalation, near-drowning, amniotic fluid embolism and, in the pandemic years, COVID-19. The <strong>Berlin definition</strong> requires acute onset within seven days of the insult, bilateral opacities on the chest X-ray not explained by effusion, collapse or nodules, respiratory failure not fully explained by heart failure or fluid overload, and a <strong>PaO₂/FiO₂ ratio</strong> of 300 mmHg (40 kPa) or less on PEEP or CPAP of at least 5 cmH₂O: mild 200 to 300, moderate 100 to 200, severe below 100. Newer definitions admit patients on high-flow nasal oxygen and an SpO₂/FiO₂ ratio where blood gases are unavailable — relevant to Ghanaian units — but the physiology is the same: flooded and collapsed alveoli shunt blood past the air, the lung that is still open is small ('the baby lung'), and the ventilator that tries to inflate the whole chest with adult-sized breaths destroys what is left.",
