            "The brain cannot be monitored by a machine at most Ghanaian bedsides; it is monitored by a nurse who examines it the same way every time and compares the result with the last. The <strong>Glasgow Coma Scale</strong> is recorded as its three components — eyes (4), verbal (5), motor (6) — never only as the total, because E2V2M5 and E4V1M4 both add to 9 and are different patients; the best response is scored, a painful stimulus is central (trapezius squeeze or supraorbital pressure) because a peripheral one tests only withdrawal, and an intubated patient is scored 'T' for verbal. A fall of two points, or any fall in the motor score, is reported immediately; the first sign of a rising intracranial pressure is a <em>change in conscious level</em> — new restlessness, drowsiness, 'not quite himself' — and the pupils change late. <strong>Pupils</strong> are examined for size (in millimetres), equality and reaction to a bright light in a darkened room: a unilaterally dilating, sluggish pupil means the third nerve is being compressed by a swelling hemisphere on that side and is a surgical emergency; bilateral fixed dilated pupils late in the course mean brainstem death unless caused by drugs (atropine, adrenaline), hypothermia or the moments after a seizure; pinpoint pupils mean opioids or a pontine lesion. <strong>Limb power</strong> is graded 0 to 5 on each side and new asymmetry reported. In the sedated patient the <strong>Richmond Agitation–Sedation Scale</strong> (+4 combative to −5 unrousable; target 0 to −2) replaces the verbal score, and the <strong>CAM-ICU</strong> screens for delirium once the RASS is −3 or lighter. Blood glucose is checked with every unexplained change in conscious level, because hypoglycaemia mimics everything.",
