            "<strong>Tiered management</strong>. Tier 0, for everyone: head of bed at 30 degrees with the head and neck in the midline (a turned head or a tight tube tie obstructs the jugular veins), no hip flexion beyond 90 degrees, normothermia (fever raises metabolic demand — treat it aggressively), normoglycaemia, normal sodium (never hyponatraemia — isotonic fluids only, no dextrose water), analgesia and sedation to prevent coughing and straining, oxygen to SpO₂ above 94 per cent, PaCO₂ 4.5 to 5.0 kPa (normal — not hyperventilated), MAP to a CPP of 60 to 70, seizure prophylaxis for one week in severe injury, bowels kept soft, and care clustered and spaced so that suction, turning and washing do not stack their pressure rises; pre-oxygenate before suction and keep passes brief. Tier 1, for sustained pressure above 22 mmHg or clinical signs of herniation: <strong>hyperosmolar therapy</strong> — mannitol 0.25 to 1 g/kg of 20 per cent solution over 15 to 20 minutes (with a urinary catheter, serum osmolality kept below 320 mOsm/kg and the fluid it diureses replaced) or hypertonic saline (for example 150 mL of 3 per cent) — drainage of cerebrospinal fluid through an external ventricular drain where one is in place, and brief <strong>hyperventilation</strong> to a PaCO₂ of 4.0 to 4.5 kPa only as a bridge to definitive treatment, because it constricts cerebral vessels and reduces blood flow. Tier 2 and 3: deeper sedation, barbiturate coma, induced hypothermia and decompressive craniectomy, in neurosurgical centres. Surgical lesions — extradural and subdural haematomas, depressed fractures — are evacuated, and the Ghanaian nurse's role in a district hospital is to protect the airway, prevent the secondary insults, and transfer the patient with a GCS below 13, a falling score, focal signs, a skull fracture or a seizure to a CT scanner and a neurosurgeon without delay, intubated if the GCS is 8 or less.",
