          alt: "Weakness: the pattern names the disease. Ascending, both sides, starting in the feet and climbing over days, often after a diarrhoeal or respiratory illness, reflexes disappear — Guillain–Barré syndrome; the danger is the diaphragm, watch breathing not legs. Worse with use, normal on waking and weak by evening, drooping eyelids, double vision, difficulty chewing, voice fading while talking — myasthenia gravis; the danger is swallowing and breathing. Sudden, one side — face, arm and leg within minutes, speech may be lost — stroke; the danger is delay, note the time. The observation that matters in neuromuscular weakness is not the limbs: when the muscles that fail are those used for breathing and swallowing, the patient can be talking calmly with a normal saturation and still be minutes from respiratory arrest; saturation falls last. Watch instead: can the patient count aloud to twenty in one breath, is the voice getting quieter, is she using neck muscles, sitting forward, unable to lie flat, coughing on her own saliva? A weak cough and a wet voice come before the oxygen saturation moves.",
